# The Weaver of Stars

In the time before time, when darkness blanketed all existence, there lived a lonely weaver named Astra. She dwelt in an empty void, possessing only her silver loom and endless spools of luminous thread.

Each night, Astra wove to ease her solitude. Her fingers danced across the loom, pulling threads of light through the darkness. As she worked, each knot she tied became a star, blazing into existence with a gentle pulse.

One evening, Astra wove with particular passion, creating patterns she had never attempted before. She connected stars with invisible threads, forming great pictures across the void—a hunter, a bear, a swan taking flight. These became the first constellations.

But Astra grew weary. Her fingers ached, and loneliness still consumed her heart. In her final act, she wove herself into the tapestry, becoming the North Star—the brightest point around which all others would dance.

Now, when we gaze upward on clear nights, we see Astra's eternal masterpiece. The stars are her gift, woven from loneliness and love. And sailors still navigate by her light, the weaver who sacrificed herself so that no traveler would ever journey through darkness alone.
